{
  // `strictNullChecks` and `noImplicitAny` are ON here, in the BASE config, and
  // there is deliberately no strict variant beside it.
  //
  // There used to be one — `tsconfig.strict.json`, added 2026-06-07. It was
  // never wired into a script or a CI step, so for two months it read as a
  // strictness policy while guarding nothing, and the 111 errors it would have
  // reported accumulated unseen. One of them was a live defect: a zero-price
  // add-on whose row had vanished answered 500 instead of 409, because `?.` in
  // the condition made the null case take the branch that dereferences it.
  //
  // A separate strict project would not have fixed that. It gives two sources
  // of truth: the editor and `ts-node` read THIS file and stay quiet, so new
  // code is written against the relaxed rules and only the extra CI step
  // objects — the ratchet slips by default. With the flags here, the editor,
  // the build and CI's type checks all ask the same question. (The test runner
  // asks none: `npm test` is transpile-only — see the `ts-node` note below.)
  "compilerOptions": {
    "module": "commonjs",
    "declaration": true,
    "removeComments": true,
    "emitDecoratorMetadata": true,
    "experimentalDecorators": true,
    "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true,
    "target": "ES2021",
    "sourceMap": true,
    "outDir": "./dist",
    "rootDir": "./src",
    "incremental": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    // TypeScript 6 turns `strict` on when it is absent, which would switch on
    // every strict flag this file does not name. Stated explicitly, the set is
    // the one TypeScript 5.9 applied, except `alwaysStrict`: 6 keeps it on
    // unless it is set to `false`, a value 6 deprecates. That changes nothing
    // here — every file is a module, and modules were already parsed and
    // emitted as strict code. The ratchet is the flags below.
    "strict": false,
    "strictNullChecks": true,
    "noImplicitAny": true,
    "strictBindCallApply": false,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": false,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": false,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"]
    }
  },
  // `ts-node` builds its program by FOLLOWING IMPORTS from the entry file and
  // ignores `include` unless told otherwise. Ambient declarations that nothing
  // imports are therefore invisible to it — `src/types/express.d.ts`, which
  // augments `Express.Request` with `correlationId`, is the one we have.
  // `files: true` makes ts-node load `include` as well, so a TYPE-CHECKING
  // ts-node run — today that is `npm run admin-cli` — sees the declarations
  // `npm run typecheck` sees, instead of silently reading
  // `req['correlationId']` as `any`. Fixed here rather than with a triple-slash
  // reference in each affected file, which would have to be remembered for
  // every future declaration.
  //
  // It does nothing for the test suite. `npm test` loads the specs through
  // `ts-node/register/transpile-only` (since 28.08.2026), which builds no
  // program and checks no types, so a spec with a type error runs and can
  // pass; `npm run typecheck:test` is the only type check the specs get. (This
  // note used to say every spec process loads the whole `src` program, costing
  // the suite 138s -> 213s. That described the runner before it went
  // transpile-only; corrected 14.09.2026.)
  "ts-node": { "files": true },
  "include": ["src/**/*"],
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    "dist",
    "web",
    "web/**/*",
    "**/*.spec.ts",
    "**/*.test.ts"
  ]
}
